ulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5a002e3-457b-5ed4-86c1-c1cd792a678a" :question/math-academy-id "q-235868" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-8x+16)+5=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+16 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-8x+16)+5 & = \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "351de637-eab5-54c7-b0bf-c59c58778c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "351de637-eab5-54c7-b0bf-c59c58778c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288385} :answer-field/id #uuid "10128d82-a9f2-566a-a758-6ccc6c76f31e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c71ef4c-c5b3-5bd0-9865-9d1ba4ecf919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c71ef4c-c5b3-5bd0-9865-9d1ba4ecf919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288677} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d3b33e7-58f7-5da4-98e7-809e4745b6f8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "173d7c13-482b-5042-aa02-eb3bdb23355c" :question/math-academy-id "q-250428" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-18x+81)+2=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-18x+81 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 9x+{9}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-9{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-18x+81)+2 & = \\\\ (x-9{)}^{2}+2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/math-academy-id "e-10008" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  + 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ab50fb-0577-520e-8649-ecef0b3f5050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y + 6)^{2} + 18" 