d/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000ea0dc-e83d-59b3-891d-03ba54bde6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000ea0dc-e83d-59b3-891d-03ba54bde6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288025} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd601eb-5637-5c7e-9cf6-b55ee889def4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290036}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aaadd0e7-cf4f-5bca-a981-28baacc544d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250466" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-10y+53.$\n\n${y}^{2}-10y+53=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+53\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $53$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+28.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ae8278a-b8a2-5f12-b4e0-1ae6448bf12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae8278a-b8a2-5f12-b4e0-1ae6448bf12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288551} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f78e4b-3042-5e44-b9db-52e393d6225e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe62bcf-2c19-5db0-aa2d-28c8e425c32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe62bcf-2c19-5db0-aa2d-28c8e425c32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289271} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c9e867-3c89-50dc-8f60-9029689722a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1f54e2d-7c34-56b8-9fdc-fc34e2e62fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-250517" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}-10x$ is written as $(x-p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dc7c32-8d37-5398-be0e-018937be178c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321196} {:answer/id #uuid "3a24431c-0458-5079-ba01-2125d8265013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321591} {:answer/id #uuid "87a74df6-aa5b-55d1-9195-ebca010ca76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\text{ }q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322227} {:answer/id #uuid "87e87cc0-c65e-58b9-830c-9fa8d6110c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\text{ }q=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322228} {:answer/id #uuid "c83b2264-9101-5cd8-bf5b-2f9a1b4b64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05dc7c32-8d37-5398-be0e-018937be178c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321196} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba493d1-ab57-5c7e-9730-deee9030cd8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9f1f3d3-135b-535b-b40c-7f56a71bf933" :question/math-academy-id "q-3662" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}-2x$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d53fe67-7a37-5135-9bda-59dc26da7eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400939} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c7488-0854-5209-9e72-78867a6a1be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400940} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec775e-a603-50a4-a821-b02cc8bdf4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-10,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400941} {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e81c67-49e3-5deb-9f3d-4bfdd5f4f433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559c184-d951-52c2-ae9b-1fd6b5cd4853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a81398bc-73ed-504a-b8b3-ef512650d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141622" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n${x - y = 4; x + 2y = 16$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 4 + y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x+2y &=16 \\\\ (4+y) + 2y &=16 \\\\ 4+3y &=16 \\\\ 3y &=12 \\\\ y &=4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into\n\n$$\nx=4+y\n$$\n\n, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= 4 + y \\\\ &= 4 + (4) \\\\ &=8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4)$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678 :topic/math-academy-id 2225 :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622 :topic/math-academy-id 356 :topic/title "Solving Two-Variable Equations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c3a49164-4f9d-597a-9dc6-abf7860aa4ba" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070261} {:answer-field/id #uuid "795f6bc6-c4b4-5520-883c-77f20cec08ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070262}] :question/id #uuid "acc0a44e-6eb6-5a95-a139-3841ec88693b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171690" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n${x + 5y = 13; y = x + 5$\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.The solution is\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b643d8b6-388a-54ab-b235-3715b075ca10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071271} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ca0af2-c9e2-50b8-aa82-29c811387109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071272} {:answer/id #uuid "db6b16f5-5009-56b0-8dbc-feec7e3509af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071273} {:answer/id #uuid "4284c760-b4f9-5590-8b60-e0effdb4b5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071274} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbb9be1-44f2-5b9e-84d5-cc040bbae729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071275}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4b7102cd-5458-52ab-8b4b-346cb2e5c0d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071270}] :question/id #uuid "963fb1bc-3f29-5c06-94a9-37168b40e1c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-4133" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n${3a + 2b = 4; - 4a + b = 2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831b8a12-3240-5543-8f0b-63a066174430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071349} {:answer/id #uuid "e71abec1-af90-564e-96cc-65f749dff99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071350} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bd14c8-f201-5c70-ba77-5489006197a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071351} {:answer/id #uuid "30891c0a-e32e-5e99-9826-2629f5f5792b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071352} {:answer/id #uuid "424e6c76-ef65-55f4-bb2c-05129500785f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071353}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff0f62d-b46f-5fed-abea-e9c339cbff76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071348}] :question/id #uuid "5c71bd6b-b9a3-5587-89f4-a14a2c760bc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-5179" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n${3p = 15; p + 2q = 9$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d447476e-6786-52c0-9a5b-aa6aa6b42f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d447476e-6786-52c0-9a5b-aa6aa6b42f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232246} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f81397-282c-55bf-a574-31b7a7eb6872" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d9ee9f-e333-5485-a7ba-ff333f3a6931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0d9ee9f-e333-5485-a7ba-ff333f3a6931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232248} :answer-field/id #uuid "e81a89a6-5e45-5c38-944f-815f8eebc873" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba03c851-f080-5b98-bcba-fc2174ffc663" :question/math-academy-id "q-167194" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f6ebe2-11a4-50a4-afb6-e54166d6b757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f6ebe2-11a4-50a4-afb6-e54166d6b757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89c06a2-d40d-59be-aba2-b51cfcb77eb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d5e96b-1699-54bb-87d3-abe3b34a48dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d5e96b-1699-54bb-87d3-abe3b34a48dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232264} :answer-field/id #uuid "80788b1d-1ccd-5c42-ae32-41ab3f383746" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232263}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d104504e-e7b5-56df-8154-499bbc1d3f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-167210" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b8ef99-22cc-5e49-9884-ae62824c5059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b8ef99-22cc-5e49-9884-ae62824c5059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232278} :answer-field/id #uuid "194751b6-0ff6-5047-a6e8-f7e3cfbe1451" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39731897-add5-5068-a83a-649fb0aa6e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39731897-add5-5068-a83a-649fb0aa6e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232280} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a21b91-803e-51b3-8b0d-859c1f623d17" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232279}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6afc1799-60d6-5f69-94a5-994731079a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-167226" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $70a+9b=600.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=75-5a \\\\ 70a+9b=600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=75-5a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70a+9b & =600 \\\\ 70a+9(75-5a) & =600 \\\\ 70a+675-45a & =600 \\\\ 25a+675 & =600 \\\\ 25a & =-75 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-3$ into $b=75-5a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =75-5(-3) \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-3,\\,b=90.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}]}
